course: biology
lesson: 237
title: Animal minds: what dogs and cats feel and understand (and do cats really chase mice?)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Dogs and cats share our homes, so we think we know their minds, but scientists keep finding surprises with careful tests.
explain: Dogs have lived with people for thousands of years and have become experts at reading us: our pointing fingers, faces and voices.
explain: Cats were never bred to work with people the way dogs were, so they know their names but often decide not to come.
explain: And Tom and Jerry? Real cats do hunt mice, but by waiting quietly and pouncing, not by long chases round the house, and a well-fed pet cat may hardly bother.
fact: Dogs make a «guilty» face when scolded even if they did nothing wrong, so the look is probably a way to calm an angry owner, not real guilt.
fact: Dogs follow human pointing very well, even as puppies, while wolves and chimpanzees find it hard.
fact: A border collie called Chaser learned the names of over 1,000 toys and worked out that a new name meant the toy she did not know; most dogs learn only a few.
fact: Real cats hunt mice by waiting quietly and pouncing, and many well-fed pet cats are not very interested in hunting.
fact: Mice will eat cheese, but they prefer grains, seeds and sweet foods, so people baiting traps usually use peanut butter.
fact: Cats purr when happy, but also when hurt or stressed, and kittens knead their mothers to get milk, a habit grown cats keep when comfortable.
fact: An experiment in Japan found cats know their own names, though they often choose not to come.
fact: Cats blink slowly at people they trust, and slow blinking back can help a cat relax.
fact: Dogs tend to wag more to the right when happy and more to the left when worried, and a stiff, high, fast wag can mean tension.
fact: Dogs sometimes catch yawns from people, more often from their own owners.
fact: Cats have a twisting reflex, using their very bendy backbone, that turns them feet-down when falling, but falls can still hurt them badly.
fact: In tests, dogs pushed and snapped at a toy dog their owner petted, but not at a bucket, which suggests a simple kind of jealousy.
fact: Dogs learn routines and read tiny signs, like shoes picked up or a coat touched, so they seem to know when it is time for a walk.
exercise: Why does a dog look «guilty» after being scolded? | To calm its angry owner, even if it did nothing wrong | Because it feels sorry about chewing that exact shoe and no other | Because it is ill | Because it wants a walk
exercise: Which animals follow a human's pointing finger best? | Dogs, even puppies | Wolves | Chimpanzees | Cats always
exercise: What did Chaser the border collie learn? | The names of over 1,000 toys | How to read books | How to talk | How to drive a car around the farm
exercise: How did Chaser find a toy she had never heard named? | She worked out that a new name meant the unknown toy | She guessed by the smell of the word as it was spoken | She picked the biggest toy | She always picked the ball
exercise: How do real cats hunt mice? | They wait quietly and then pounce | They chase them round the house for hours like Tom | They set traps | They dig the mice out with tools
exercise: What do mice prefer to cheese? | Grains, seeds and sweet foods | Raw fish left out in the kitchen | Lettuce leaves and nothing else | Nothing, cheese is their favourite
exercise: When do cats purr? | When happy, but also when hurt or stressed | Only when they are happy and nothing else | Only when hungry | Only when asleep
exercise: Why do cats knead with their paws? | Kittens do it to get milk, and grown cats keep the habit when comfortable | To sharpen their claws only | To dig a hole in your lap | To warn you to go away
exercise: Do cats know their own names? | Yes, but they often choose not to come | No, cats can never learn any names at all | Only if the name is short and said loudly | Only kittens do, grown cats soon forget them
exercise: What does a cat's slow blink at you show? | It trusts you | It is cross with you | It wants to fight | Its eyes hurt in the light
exercise: Which way does a happy dog tend to wag more? | To the right | To the left | Straight up and down | It does not wag when happy
exercise: Does a wagging tail always mean a friendly dog? | No, a stiff, high, fast wag can mean tension | Yes, always | Yes, if the tail is long | No, a wagging tail always means the dog is very angry
exercise: From whom do dogs catch yawns most? | Their own owners | Strangers in the street | Cats in the same house | Other dogs only, never people
exercise: What helps a falling cat turn feet-down? | A twisting reflex using its bendy backbone | Its tail, spinning round like a propeller | Its whiskers catching the wind as it falls | Nothing, it lands any way
exercise: What did dogs do when their owner petted a toy dog? | Pushed and snapped at it, but not at a bucket | Ignored it completely | Petted it too | Ran away and hid under the bed until it was put away
exercise: How does a dog seem to know it is walk time? | It learns routines and reads tiny signs like shoes picked up | It reads the clock | It hears the lead talking | It guesses by magic, because dogs have a sixth sense for walks
test: In Tom and Jerry, Tom chases Jerry round the house in every episode. What would a real pet cat more likely do? | Wait quietly and pounce, or not bother if it is well fed | Chase the mouse round the house for hours with a frying pan | Make friends and eat cheese together | Set a trap with cheese
test: A cartoon mouse risks a trap for a big wedge of cheese. What would a real mouse go for first? | Peanut butter or seeds | The cheese, every time | A lump of salt | Nothing, mice do not eat
test: «my dog chewed my shoe and looked SO guilty, he knows he was bad» What is the science? | The look is probably to calm you down, not guilt about the shoe | Yes, he feels sorry about that exact shoe and remembers chewing it | Dogs never make faces | He wants another shoe
test: «my cat ignores me when I call, she doesn't even know her name» What is probably true? | She knows it, but chooses not to come | Cats cannot hear names | Her name is too long for her | She is deaf in both ears
test: A cartoon cat falls off a roof, lands on its feet and walks away fine. What is the real picture? | Cats twist feet-down, but falls can still hurt them badly | Cats can never get hurt by falling, from any height at all | Cats always land on their backs | Cats can fly a little
test: «dog's going mental at the door, it's 5 o'clock, how'd he know» What is the best answer? | He learned the routine and reads little signs | Dogs can tell the time by looking at clocks | It is pure magic | He smelled the clock
test: A friend says a purring cat is always happy. What is wrong? | Cats also purr when hurt or stressed | Nothing, purring only ever means happy | Cats purr only when they are angry with you | Only kittens purr, never grown-up cats
test: «my dog yawned when dad yawned lol coincidence» What might it really be? | Dogs sometimes catch yawns, most from their own people | A coincidence, dogs never catch yawns | The dog was copying the TV | Dad caught it from the dog first, always
test: What would happen if you pointed at a hidden ball for a wolf instead of a dog? | The wolf would usually find it hard to follow the point | The wolf would find it much faster than any dog ever could | The wolf would bring you the ball | The wolf would point back
test: Count them: Tom the cat, Spike the bulldog and Jerry the mouse in a real kitchen. How many ears and how many tails? | 6 ears and 3 tails | 3 ears and 3 tails | 6 ears and 6 tails | 12 ears and 3 tails
test: Count them: 2 dogs and 3 cats come to the vet. How many legs? | 20 legs | 10 legs | 16 legs | 24 legs
test: «my dog shoved my hand off the neighbour's puppy, is he jealous??» What do tests suggest? | Dogs may feel a simple kind of jealousy | Dogs cannot feel anything like that at all | He is trying to shake hands with the puppy | He thinks the puppy is something good to eat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the «guilty» expression exhibited by a dog upon being reprimanded? | It is displayed irrespective of actual misconduct and probably serves to appease the owner | It constitutes a reliable admission of the specific misdeed | It is never observed in domestic dogs | It is displayed only by dogs of working breeds
exam: Identify the manner in which a domestic cat ordinarily captures a mouse. | By waiting motionless and subsequently pouncing | By prolonged pursuit throughout the dwelling | By the setting of baited traps | By excavating the burrow with implements
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the response of a cat to its own name. | The cat recognises its name but frequently elects not to respond | The cat is incapable of distinguishing its name | The cat responds invariably and immediately | The cat recognises only names consisting of a single syllable
exam: Ascertain the principal significance of a stiff, high and rapid wag of the tail in a dog. | It may indicate tension rather than friendliness | It invariably indicates delight | It indicates that the dog is asleep and dreaming | It indicates hunger alone
